'''OnePlus Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd.''' ({{zh|c=一加科技|p=Yījiā Kējì|l=one plus technology}}), doing business as '''OnePlus''', is a Chinese [[consumer electronics]] manufacturer headquartered in [[Shenzhen]], [[Guangdong]], [[China]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Privacy Policy |url=https://www.oneplus.com/global/legal/privacy-policy |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190526122902/https://www.oneplus.com/global/legal/privacy-policy |archive-date=26 May 2019 |access-date=10 October 2019 |publisher=OnePlus |quote=Postal address: F18, Block C, Tairan Building, Tairan 8th Road, Chegongmiao, Futian District, Shenzhen, China, Zip Code: 518040}} // [https://account.oneplus.com/cn/agreement_privacy Address in Chinese] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191009184356/https://account.oneplus.com/cn/agreement_privacy |date=9 October 2019 }}: "快递地址：中国深圳市福田区车公庙泰然八路泰然大厦C座18楼，邮编: 518040" ("Express address: Floor 18, Block C, Tairan Building, Tairan 8th Road, Chegongmiao, Futian District, Shenzhen, China, Zip code: 518040")</ref> It is a subsidiary of [[Oppo]].

OnePlus was founded by [[Pete Lau]] and [[Carl Pei]] on 16 December 2013 to develop a high-end flagship smartphone running [[CyanogenMod#History and development|Cyanogen OS]] that would come to be known as the [[OnePlus One]]. OnePlus would continue to release smartphones afterwards. In 2020, OnePlus released the [[OnePlus Nord]], its first mid-range smartphone since the [[OnePlus X]] in 2015. Pei would oversee the design and marketing of OnePlus' products until his departure from the company in October 2020, going on to found the consumer electronics manufacturer [[Nothing (company)|Nothing]].

{{Short description|Autonomous computer subsystem}}
{{Use American English|date=October 2025}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=October 2025}}
{{Update section|date=March 2026|reason=The Intel Management Engine has been renamed as of ~9 years ago to Intel CSME. I cite my sources in the "It was renamed 2017 to Intel CSME" discussion post.}}
[[File:Priv rings.svg|thumb|[[Privilege ring]]s for the [[x86]] architecture. The ME is colloquially categorized as ring −3, below [[System Management Mode]] (ring −2) and the [[hypervisor]] (ring −1), all running at a higher privilege level than the kernel (ring 0).]]

The '''Intel Converged Security and Management Engine''' ('''CSME''')<ref>{{Cite web | title=Intel® Converged Security and Management Engine Version Detection Tool (Intel® CSMEVDT) | url=https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/download/19392/intel-converged-security-and-management-engine-version-detection-tool-intel-csmevdt.html | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220121215803/https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/download/19392/intel-converged-security-and-management-engine-version-detection-tool-intel-csmevdt.html | access-date=2026-09-09 | archive-date=2022-01-21}}</ref>, {{Dubious span|text=also known as the Intel Manageability Engine|reason=Intel "Managability" Engine?|date=September 2025}},<ref name="intelamt">{{cite web |url=https://software.intel.com/en-us/articles/getting-started-with-intel-active-management-technology-amt |title=Getting Started with Intel Active Management Technology (Intel AMT) |first=Joseph E. |last=Oster |date=September 3, 2019 |access-date=September 22, 2020 |publisher=Intel |archive-date=August 5, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160805025427/https://software.intel.com/en-us/articles/getting-started-with-intel-active-management-technology-amt |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="intel-amt-me">{{cite web|url=https://software.intel.com/en-us/blogs/2011/12/14/intelr-amt-and-the-intelr-me|title=Intel AMT and the Intel ME|publisher=Intel|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190221093441/https://software.intel.com/en-us/blogs/2011/12/14/intelr-amt-and-the-intelr-me/|url-status=usurped|archive-date=February 21, 2019}}</ref> is an autonomous subsystem that has been incorporated in virtually all of [[Intel]]'s [[Central processing unit|processor]] [[chipset]]s since 2008.<ref name="intelamt" /><ref>{{cite web |title=Frequently Asked Questions for the Intel Converged Security and Management Engine Verification Utility |url=https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000005974/software/chipset-software.html |quote=Built into many Intel Chipset–based platforms is a small, low-power computer subsystem called the Intel Converged Security Management Engine (Intel CSME). |access-date=November 7, 2017 |archive-date=November 8, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171108093924/https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/support/articles/000005974/software/chipset-software.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="eff1">{{cite web |last1=Portnoy |first1=Erica |last2=Eckersley |first2=Peter |date=May 8, 2017 |title=Intel's Management Engine is a security hazard, and users need a way to disable it |url=https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2017/05/intels-management-engine-security-hazard-and-users-need-way-disable-it |publisher=Electronic Frontier Foundation |access-date=February 21, 2020 |archive-date=May 8, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170508201112/https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2017/05/intels-management-engine-security-hazard-and-users-need-way-disable-it |url-status=live }}</ref> It is located in the [[Platform Controller Hub]] of modern Intel [[motherboards]].

The Intel Converged Security and Management Engine always runs as long as the motherboard is receiving power, even when the computer is turned off. This issue can be mitigated with the deployment of a hardware device which is able to disconnect all connections to [[mains power]] as well as all internal forms of energy storage. The [[Electronic Frontier Foundation]] and some security researchers have voiced concern that the Converged Security and Management Engine is a [[Backdoor (computing)|backdoor]].<ref name="eff1" />

Intel's main competitor, [[AMD]], has incorporated the equivalent [[AMD Secure Technology]] (formally called Platform Security Processor) in virtually all of its post-2013 CPUs.

{{Short description|Activity of computer programming for expressive purposes}}
{{refimprove|date=March 2014}}
[[File:Creative coding breakout modification.png|thumb|upright|A heavily modified version of the classic 1980s video game ''[[Breakout (video game)|Breakout]]'' produces visually interesting glitches.]]
'''Creative coding''' is a type of [[computer programming]] in which the goal is to create something expressive instead of something functional. It is used to create live visuals and for [[VJing]], as well as creating visual art and design, entertainment (e.g. [[Video game|video games]]), art installations, projections and [[projection mapping]], sound art, advertising, product [[prototype]]s, and much more.

{{short description|none}}
<!-- "none" is preferred when the title is sufficiently descriptive; see [[WP:SDNONE]] -->
The people of [[Laos]] have a rich literary tradition dating back at least six hundred years, with the oral and storytelling traditions of its peoples dating back much earlier.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Hundius|first1=Harold|title=The Digital Library of Lao Manuscripts|journal=The Journal of Lao Studies|date=2011|volume=2|issue=2|page=68|url=http://www.laostudies.org/system/files/subscription/JLS-v2-i2-Nov2011-hundius-wharton.pdf|accessdate=7 February 2016}}</ref> Lao literature refers to the written productions of Laotian peoples, its émigrés, and to Lao-language works. In Laos today there are over forty-seven recognized ethnic groups, with the [[Lao Loum]] comprising the majority group.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Goudineau|first1=Yves|title=Laos and Ethnic Minority Cultures: Promoting Heritage|date=2003|publisher=UNESCO Publishing|location=Paris|isbn=92-3-103891-5|page=14|url=http://unesdoc.unesco.org/images/0013/001309/130985e.pdf|accessdate=14 February 2016}}</ref>  [[Lao language|Lao]] (part of the [[Lao-Tai Languages|Lao-Tai]] family) is officially recognized as the national language, but owing to the ethnic diversity of the country the '''literature of Laos''' can generally be grouped according to four [[ethnolinguistic]] families: Lao–Tai ([[Tai–Kadai]]); [[Mon–Khmer]] (Austroasiatic); [[Hmong–Mien]] (Miao–Yao), and [[Sino-Tibetan languages|Sino-Tibetan]] (primarily Tibeto-Burman).<ref>{{cite book|last1=Chamberlain|first1=James|title=The Literary Heritage of Laos: Preservation, Dissemination and Research Perspectives|date=2005|publisher=National Library of Laos|location=Vientiane|page=326|url=http://www.laomanuscripts.net/downloads/literaryheritageoflaos24_chamberlain_en.pdf|access-date=2016-02-14|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202001828/http://www.laomanuscripts.net/downloads/literaryheritageoflaos24_chamberlain_en.pdf|archive-date=2017-02-02|url-status=dead}}</ref> As an inland crossroads of [[Southeast Asia]] the political history of Laos has been complicated by frequent warfare and colonial conquests by European and regional rivals.

